 "verdict": {
  "what_the_screen_tests": "Both arms have a complete support (|S| = 300/300) under both bit orders. The reconstruction diagonalises H(U_rec) on the whole sector, using the exact ED vector phi(U_rec) and E0(U_rec), so mbar equals the exact moments of H(U_rec) (difference 0.0, key data_free_control). The screen residual |mbar - m(U=4)|/m(U=4) is then a function of U_rec alone. Either arm's counts, read in either bit order, give the same screen as a data-free control (S = the full sector, no device data); the maximum difference is max_abs_diff_any_device_screen_vs_data_free_pct = 0.0. Reconstructing the correct arm with U_rec = 3.5 makes the screen fire, and reconstructing the detuned arm with U_rec = 4 makes it pass (key cross_assignment). The 'detuned arm caught' outcome therefore detects which Hamiltonian the analyst used, not the detuned device schedule. The ROC label (spectral distance > 0.05 from the U=4 ED spectrum) is also a function of U_rec, and it coincides with the arm label. All 200 bootstrap replicates of an arm give the same value (one distinct value per arm). AUC = 1 for m0 too, because its residuals are 0 and 1e-15 (rounding), not because m0 discriminates. The AUCs therefore carry no information about the device. Device data enter only through S, and only when S is incomplete (key coverage_scan): below a few thousand retained shots, the screen then responds to missing support.",
  "bit_order": "The notebook read bitstrings with int(bs[::-1],2). That map sends qubit p to bit 11-p (up<->down, site i<->5-i), so it maps the (N=7, Sz=+1) sector onto (N=7, Sz=-1), and every noiseless shot is rejected: the noiseless in-sector mass is 0 for all 14 circuits. Every shot retained by the as-run analysis (13527 and 12969) is therefore a device error with (N=7, Sz=-1) in the true reading, a set equality checked shot by shot. With the correct map int(bs,2), retention is 73364/245000 = 29.9% and 73475/245000 = 30.0%. The support is again complete, so every screen and AUC output is unchanged.",
  "device_data_carry_the_schedule": "This is not a moment test. Read in the correct order, the retained counts favour their own schedule in pooled log-likelihood: +0.215 per shot (towards U=4) in the correct arm and -0.096 (towards U=3.5) in the detuned arm. Among circuits k = 1..6, the counts are closer in TV to their own schedule in 5/6 (correct arm) and 4/6 (detuned arm). The device-vs-noiseless TV is 0.20-0.39 per circuit. The device output therefore carries a weak, noisy imprint of the detuning, and the moment screen as built does not use it.",
  "paper": "Not as a result. The run is not a hardware true positive or true negative of the moment screen, because the verdict does not depend on device data at complete support, and the numbers printed on 2026-08-21 came from mis-ordered bitstrings. If the paper lists every device job, the run fits as one execution-record line: two ibm_fez Sampler jobs (ids), 2026-08-21, L=6, 7 circuits x 35 000 shots, 69 QPU-s each. One sentence of what it shows would accompany it: the support was complete in both arms (correct-order retention 29.9% / 30.0%), so a support-based reconstruction with exact amplitudes returns the exact moments of whichever Hamiltonian is assumed, and the screen cannot see the device. Otherwise the run belongs only in the repository (data/heron_gatetp_2026-08-21/, this script, this JSON). No AUC, true-positive or noise-survival claim from this run should appear anywhere."
 },
